{% set local_problem %}
<p>Most Madison homes have a downspout problem they don't even know about. Builder-grade downspouts are code-minimum: 2x3 inch, positioned at whatever spacing seemed convenient during construction. That setup handles light rain fine. Heavy rain? Not so much.</p>

<p>When downspouts can't keep up, water backs up in your gutters and overflows. But even worse: many downspouts dump water right next to the foundation. On Madison's clay soil, that water doesn't absorb — it pools, saturates, and eventually finds its way into your basement or crawlspace.</p>

<p>We see this constantly in <a href="/recent-projects/">Madison subdivisions</a>: homes with "working" gutter systems that are actually funneling thousands of gallons of water directly toward the foundation every year. The family notices basement moisture and blames the clay soil. The actual problem is downspouts dumping water 18 inches from the house.</p>

<p><strong>The builder-grade reality:</strong> Most Madison homes built since 2000 have 2x3 downspouts spaced 50-60+ feet apart, with 12-18 inch splash blocks that do basically nothing. That's technically code-compliant. It's also inadequate for our rainfall.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>A good downspout system has three components: enough capacity, proper positioning, and effective extensions.</p>

<p><strong>Capacity:</strong> You need one downspout for every 35-40 linear feet of gutter run, max. And they should be 3x4 inch, not 2x3. The 3x4 size handles roughly twice the flow rate — critical during 2-inch-an-hour storms.</p>

<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> Downspouts go where water naturally collects — typically corners and low points in the gutter run. Bad positioning means water has to travel too far, slowing the system down.</p>

<p><strong>Extensions:</strong> Water needs to discharge 4-10 feet from your foundation at minimum. Better: underground drains that route water 15+ feet away. Those 18-inch splash blocks? Decorative at best.</p>

<p><strong>Our honest take:</strong> If you're having overflow problems, the cheapest fix is often just adding downspouts. One or two additional downspouts (maybe $200-400) can solve problems that seem like they require a complete gutter replacement.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've been solving drainage problems in <a href="/recent-projects/">Madison</a> for over 20 years. We understand how clay soil, high water tables, and intense rainfall combine to create foundation problems. Our downspout solutions are designed for these specific challenges.</p>

<p>As your neighbors 10 miles away, we're here when you need us. From simple downspout extensions to complete system redesigns, we'll find the right solution for your situation and budget.</p>

<p><strong>Warning from experience:</strong> Those corrugated flex pipes people bury in their yards? They clog within 2-3 years. Every time. The corrugations trap debris and roots grow into them. If you want buried drainage, use smooth PVC. We dig up failed flex pipe installations constantly.</p>

<p><strong>Our opinion:</strong> Before you spend $2,000+ on new gutters because of overflow, let us look at your downspouts. Half the overflow problems we see are downspout issues — not enough of them, wrong size, bad positioning. That's a much cheaper fix.</p>
